
Someone asked me recently about the time scale for interconverting
conformers, e.g., if a synthesis generates one conformer how long
does it take to convert to another conformer given different barrier
heights. Here was my reply. This is the origin of the rule-of-thumb
that trans<->gauche transitions for alkanes should occur every 10-20
ps in a MD simulation. Bill

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
From TS theory, the first-order rate constant is

k = kappa(kB*T/h)exp(-delGact/RT) = 6x10^12 exp(-delGact/RT) in sec^-1 at T =
298K

then for a first-order process (conformer A -> conformer B)

half-life = t1/2 = (ln 2)/k

So at T = 298 K

     Barrier Height (kcal/mol)      t1/2 (sec)
             3                      17 x 10^-12  (17 ps)
             5                      4.8 x 10^-10 (0.5 ns)
            10                      2.0 x 10^-6  (2 us)
            15                      0.0084       (8 ms)
            20                      35           (0.5 min)
            30                      (6916 days)
           100                      2.8 x 10^59  (forever)

Conformer B will begin to build-up and revert to A; however, after ca.
10 half-lifes the system will be close to equilibrium.

Bill

